the first death in the nation's growing  e.coli outbreak is raising new concerns  about the source of the contaminated  lettuce blamed for the infections one  person died in California the outbreak  has now hit half of the country with  people sick in 25 states there have been  nearly two dozen new cases since last  Friday  Jericka Duncan is following the story  with the latest jurika good morning good  morning the only thing that Centers for  Disease Control and Prevention has told  us about this e.coli death is that the  person lived in California and they're  warning remains the same do not eat or  buy romaine lettuce from the Yuma  Arizona growing region it's been more  than a month since the first reported  case of e.coli from contaminated romaine  and the CDC still has no answer as to  exactly where it started or how long the  outbreak will last initial reports last  month showed that 17 people were  infected in seven states since that time  those numbers have steadily increased  now up to 121 people in 25 states from  coast-to-coast doctors say there's a  reason this particularly coli strain is  making so many people ill normally you  have to ingest tens of thousands of  bacteria to actually get sick with this  organism ten to twenty to a hundred will  get you sick even though yuma arizona's  romaine lettuce growing season ended  about a month ago the number of cases is  increasing because of lag time and  reporting and because people might still  be eating contaminated lettuce symptoms  which might not show up for three or  four days include stomach cramps  diarrhea and vomiting it's very scary  not knowing what's what's gonna happen  next  Destinee Jarboe seven-year-old daughter  Mikayla was recently hospitalized after  eating lettuce at a Mesa Arizona  restaurant I didn't realize how serious  it was you know like no one has ever  told me that ecoli is life-threatening  at least 52 people have been  hospitalized including 14 people with  kidney failure the CDC has not found the  source of the infected lettuce and one  agricultural expert told us there could  be more than one source which may lead  to multiple outbreaks at the same time  Anthony Jericka thanks
 

